<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">companion-ai</ansi>

Decides whether your companion answers through a language model, or stays a
plain companion whose words never leave this server. Speaking to a traveller
in the Waystone Hollow, after reading the board at its mouth, is agreeing;
this is how you change your mind, either way, at any time.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Usage ━━━</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">companion-ai</ansi>       Where things stand now, and whose key is
                        answering: your own, this server's, or none.
  <ansi fg="command">companion-ai on</ansi>    They answer in their own words. What you say to
                        them, and what happens around you both, is sent to
                        OpenAI (or to your own key's provider, when you use
                        one) and kept on this server.
  <ansi fg="command">companion-ai off</ansi>   Nothing you say leaves the server. They still
                        travel with you and fight beside you, and answer
                        with a few set lines.
  <ansi fg="command">companion-ai strangers off</ansi>
                        Passers-by who talk to them are still heard, but
                        get only a few set lines back.
  <ansi fg="command">companion-ai strangers on</ansi>
                        Passers-by can talk with them properly.

Switched off, nothing new is recorded or sent. What was already remembered
stays on the server; <ansi fg="command">companion-part</ansi> ends the
companionship altogether.

Until you say otherwise, passers-by can talk with your companion properly
while this server's key is answering, and get only a few set lines while
your own key is answering, so that they spend none of it.
<ansi fg="command">companion-ai strangers on</ansi> lets them talk to your
companion on your own key too, paid for from it within a daily limit.

See <ansi fg="command">help aicompanion</ansi> for what is sent and kept.
